<h2>Where the power sits in the appellate scheme</h2>
<p>A criminal appeal begins with a petition of appeal accompanied by the judgment under Section 423 BNSS (382 CrPC), or through the jail route under Section 424 BNSS (383 CrPC) for appellants in custody. Section 425 then interposes a judicial filter: the appellate court examines the petition and the judgment and asks a single question — is there sufficient ground for interfering? If the answer is plainly no, the court may dismiss the appeal summarily, saving the parties and the system a full hearing that could only end one way.</p>

<h2>The safeguards built into the section</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>A hearing first.</strong> An appeal presented in the ordinary course is not dismissed summarily unless the appellant or counsel has had a reasonable opportunity of being heard in support of it. The threshold decision is made after argument, however brief.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Jail appeals protected.</strong> An appellant who has appealed from jail is likewise entitled to an opportunity of being heard, except where the court considers the appeal frivolous — a deliberately narrow exception.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Reasons on the record.</strong> Where a court other than the High Court dismisses an appeal summarily, it must record its reasons. A one-line rejection by a Sessions Court invites interference on that ground alone.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The record can be called.</strong> Before deciding, the court may send for the trial record. Where the petition raises any arguable point on the evidence, the sounder course — and the usual one — is to call for the record and admit the appeal.</div>
</div>

<h2>Summary dismissal versus the other endings</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Summary dismissal.</strong> A merits decision at the threshold: the court has read the petition and judgment, heard the appellant, and concluded that no sufficient ground exists. It ends the appeal, subject to challenge before the higher forum.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Dismissal after full hearing.</strong> The appeal is admitted, the record called, the State heard under the later provisions of the chapter, and the appeal decided by a reasoned judgment under Section 427 BNSS (386 CrPC) after complete argument.</div>
</div>
<p>A third category does not lawfully exist: dismissal for default. Criminal appeals are not subject to the civil discipline of non-prosecution. Courts have repeatedly held that an appeal against conviction cannot be dismissed merely because the appellant or counsel is absent; the court must decide it on the merits, if necessary after appointing amicus curiae. An order styled as a dismissal for non-prosecution in a criminal appeal is a recognised irregularity.</p>

<h2>Why admission-stage advocacy decides cases</h2>
<p>The summary-dismissal stage compresses the whole appeal into minutes. What the judge has is the petition of appeal and the judgment; what the judge is looking for is one arguable reason to call for the record. The drafting consequence is direct: grounds of appeal should lead with the strongest, most concrete infirmity — a contradiction on a material particular, a reliance on inadmissible material, a sentencing error — rather than boilerplate about the judgment being &ldquo;bad in law and on facts&rdquo;. At the hearing, counsel&rsquo;s task is not to win the appeal but to cross the modest threshold of &ldquo;sufficient ground for interfering&rdquo;.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p>Annex a legible copy of the judgment and, where available, the material depositions the grounds rely upon — the judge can only act on what is before the court.</p>
  <p>Open with the single best point, stated as a specific error with a page reference, not as a conclusion.</p>
  <p>Where the appellant is in custody, couple the admission argument with the application for suspension of sentence or bail, since both turn on the same arguable points.</p>
  <p>If the court is unpersuaded, seek a short date to place the record rather than accept dismissal on an incomplete file.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">Section 425 BNSS is the gate through which every criminal appeal must pass, and most pass it easily — the power is used against appeals that disclose nothing arguable, not against appeals that are merely difficult. The practical lesson is to treat the first listing as a real hearing: a well-drafted petition and ten focused minutes of argument are usually all that separates admission from a summary end.</div>
